import{_ as s,o as a,c as t,a3 as i}from"./chunks/framework.B9qR_F1m.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Risk: ripeat assess","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"assess.md","filePath":"assess.md"}'),n={name:"assess.md"};function o(r,e,l,d,h,p){return a(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i(`<h1 id="risk-ripeat-assess" tabindex="-1">Risk: <code>ripeat assess</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#risk-ripeat-assess" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Risk: \`ripeat assess\`&quot;">​</a></h1><p><code>ripeat assess</code> says how risky a plan is, before you run it. It is plain rules, not AI: the same plan always gets the same answer, every reason is written out, and you can disagree with a rule and read it below. It changes nothing on the machine.</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">ripeat</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> assess</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> git</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> node@20</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> ripgrep</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">      # tools named on the command line</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">ripeat</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> assess</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --file</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> ripeat.yaml</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">       # a profile</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">ripeat</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> assess</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --accounts</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> ripeat.policy.yaml</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">   # also: users, groups and admin rights ([Users and groups](./policy))</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">ripeat</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> assess</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --json</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> ...</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">               # the same report for programs</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">ripeat</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> assess</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --fail-on</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> high</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> ...</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">       # exit with an error at or above a level (medium or high), for scripts</span></span></code></pre></div><p>It uses the same planning rules as <code>ripeat plan</code> (fallbacks, dependencies, <code>ripeat.lock</code>, the administrator policy, <code>--scope</code>). It assesses requested tools independently so it can report all blockers; the full install still resolves the complete request together.</p><h2 id="what-a-report-shows" tabindex="-1">What a report shows <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-a-report-shows" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What a report shows&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>Risk: MEDIUM   1 to install · 2 already managed · 1 left alone</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  RISK      TOOL     HOW             WHY</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  -         git      already managed by ripeat, skipped</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  MEDIUM    ripgrep  github_release  requested</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                     - downloads a program from a third party: its SHA-256 is</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                       verified, but ripeat cannot vouch for the project itself</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  -         docker   already on this machine, left alone</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>What would run, without administrator rights:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  download the latest BurntSushi/ripgrep release ...</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Can it be undone? Yes: \`ripeat rollback\` removes what this run installs</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The first line is the whole story: the level, then how many tools would be installed, are already managed, are left alone, or are <strong>for you to install by hand</strong> (a <code>manual</code> recipe whose tool is missing: shown as &quot;by hand&quot;, with its note, no risk level, and <code>&quot;status&quot;: &quot;manual&quot;</code> in <code>--json</code>; a tool that needs one is reported as blocked). Long reasons are wrapped under the tool. On a terminal the levels are coloured (green LOW, yellow MEDIUM, red HIGH and BLOCKED); in a pipe, in a file, for an AI assistant, with <code>--json</code>, or when the <code>NO_COLOR</code> variable is set, the text is plain.</p><p>If any requested tool is blocked, the report says the full request cannot be installed yet. Other tools are assessed individually and their commands are previews, not a promise that the complete plan can run. Resolve the listed reasons, then assess the profile again; undo information applies only if the available steps are later installed.</p><ul><li><strong>Risk:</strong> the highest level among the steps that would run: LOW, MEDIUM, HIGH, or BLOCKED.</li><li><strong>Each tool:</strong> its level (<code>none</code> for a tool that is left alone, since there is nothing to rate), install method, why it is in the plan (requested, or a dependency of another tool), and the reasons for its level. Tools already installed are listed as left alone and add no risk.</li><li><strong>Version warnings:</strong> a tool already on the machine is left alone, even when you asked for a different version (<code>node@20</code> while <code>24.15.0</code> is installed). The report says so in the tool&#39;s line (&quot;it is 24.15.0, NOT the 20 you asked for&quot;) and in a note, and <code>ripeat plan</code> and <code>ripeat install</code> carry the same warning. ripeat does not claim a version is in place when it is not. If it cannot detect the installed version, it says nothing either way.</li><li><strong>The commands:</strong> exactly what would run, split into those that need administrator rights and those that do not.</li><li><strong>Whether it can be undone</strong> (<code>ripeat rollback</code>), and notes such as lockfile pins that were not used.</li></ul><p>A recipe that came from a recipe pack (<a href="./taps">Recipe packs (taps)</a>) is rated like one you wrote yourself, and its reason names the pack (&quot;the recipe comes from the recipe pack acme, not shipped with ripeat&quot;).</p><h2 id="the-rules" tabindex="-1">The rules <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-rules" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The rules&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each step gets the highest level that applies, and the plan gets the highest of its steps.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Fact about the step</th><th>Level</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Forbidden by the administrator policy, no recipe, or install method unavailable here</td><td><strong>BLOCKED</strong> (cannot run)</td></tr><tr><td>A classic-confinement snap (<code>classic: true</code>), which has broad system access</td><td><strong>HIGH</strong></td></tr><tr><td>A <code>shell</code> command in a recipe <strong>you added</strong> (including an approved draft)</td><td><strong>HIGH</strong></td></tr><tr><td>A download ripeat cannot verify (<code>deb_url</code>, no checksum) in a recipe <strong>you added</strong></td><td><strong>HIGH</strong></td></tr><tr><td>An <code>installer</code> that is a <code>.pkg</code>, <code>.msi</code> or <code>.exe</code> (runs the vendor&#39;s installer as administrator), or any installer with <code>unsigned: true</code></td><td><strong>HIGH</strong></td></tr><tr><td>A policy file that grants administrator rights (<code>admin: true</code>)</td><td><strong>HIGH</strong></td></tr><tr><td>A <code>shell</code> command, or an unverified <code>deb_url</code> download, in a recipe <strong>shipped with ripeat</strong></td><td>MEDIUM</td></tr><tr><td>A third-party program downloaded from GitHub or a URL, SHA-256 verified</td><td>MEDIUM</td></tr><tr><td>An <code>installer</code> that is a <code>.dmg</code> (copies an app, runs nothing; SHA-256 and signature verified)</td><td>MEDIUM</td></tr><tr><td>A package that needs administrator rights (for example <code>apt</code>, <code>dnf</code>), in any recipe</td><td>MEDIUM</td></tr><tr><td>Creating users or groups without admin rights</td><td>MEDIUM</td></tr><tr><td>A step that cannot be undone (a <code>shell</code> recipe with no <code>uninstall</code>)</td><td>noted; part of the shell rating</td></tr><tr><td>A package for your user only, from the system&#39;s package manager (<code>brew</code>, <code>scoop</code>, ...)</td><td>LOW</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Why it is shaped like this:</p><ul><li><strong>Added recipes are judged more strictly</strong> for the things where the recipe itself supplies what runs: a shell command, or a download. Recipes shipped with ripeat were written and tested by the project; one you added (or an AI drafted and you approved) has not been.</li><li><strong>A package-manager recipe is not escalated</strong> just because you added it: it only supplies a package name, and ripeat builds the command, so <code>apt install &lt;name&gt;</code> stays MEDIUM whoever wrote the recipe.</li><li><strong>A verified download is MEDIUM, not LOW:</strong> the SHA-256 shows the file is the one that was published, not that the project behind it is trustworthy.</li><li><strong>Admin rights make a package MEDIUM,</strong> because the command runs as root.</li></ul><h2 id="what-ripeat-does-with-the-level" tabindex="-1">What ripeat does with the level <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-ripeat-does-with-the-level" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What ripeat does with the level&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong><code>ripeat install</code></strong> prints a short &quot;Risk:&quot; summary with the reasons, then: <ul><li>LOW and MEDIUM go to the normal question, &quot;Install N tools as shown above?&quot; (<a href="./install-and-rollback">Install, uninstall, rollback</a>).</li><li><strong>HIGH</strong> runs only if you say so. In a terminal ripeat shows the reasons and asks <code>[y/N]</code>. Without a terminal (a script, or an AI client) it refuses unless you pass <code>--accept-risk</code>. Nothing is changed when it refuses. <code>--yes</code> answers the normal question but never this one: only <code>--accept-risk</code> (or a yes to the HIGH-risk question) lets a HIGH plan run, and approving it counts as the install confirmation too. <code>ripeat resume</code> does the same, except that a plan you already accepted before a restart is not asked about again.</li><li><code>ripeat plan</code> and <code>--dry-run</code> show the risk but are never blocked.</li></ul></li><li><strong><code>uninstall</code>, <code>rollback</code> and <code>upgrade</code></strong> are not gated: removing is never blocked, and <code>upgrade</code> only touches tools ripeat already installed.</li><li>The administrator policy (<a href="./admin-policy">Administrator policy</a>) is separate and stricter: it forbids outright, and nothing, <code>--accept-risk</code> included, overrides it.</li></ul><h2 id="limits" tabindex="-1">Limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Limits&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>It judges what a step <em>does</em>, not whether a project or a package name is trustworthy. A MEDIUM download is verified, not vouched for.</li><li>It does not ask the package manager to simulate the install, so it cannot yet tell that a package would pull in or remove other packages.</li><li><code>upgrade</code> is not rated by <code>assess</code>: upgrades cannot be rolled back (see <a href="./upgrades">Versions and upgrades</a>).</li><li>The levels are judgment calls. If one looks wrong to you, the rule and its reason are in the report.</li></ul>`,19)])])}const g=s(n,[["render",o]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
